<h2 id="knee-exercises-osteoarthritis">Knee Exercises for Osteoarthritis</h2>
<p>If you have knee osteoarthritis (OA), the priority is loaded strengthening of the quadriceps and glutes combined with low-impact aerobic work. NICE recommends a structured 12-week exercise programme as a <strong>core treatment</strong> — equal in importance to weight management and education. The straight leg raise, wall sit, sit-to-stand and stationary cycling described above are the most studied movements for knee OA and the safest starting points for most adults. Begin at 2 sessions per week, building to 3–4 over six weeks. For a deeper dive into condition-specific pacing, read our <a href="/conditions/osteoarthritis/exercises">osteoarthritis exercises guide</a> and the broader <a href="/conditions/osteoarthritis/treatment">osteoarthritis treatment overview</a>.</p>

<h2 id="knee-exercises-rheumatoid-arthritis">Knee Exercises for Rheumatoid Arthritis</h2>
<p>Knee involvement in rheumatoid arthritis (RA) calls for a gentler progression and clear flare rules. During an active flare, swap strengthening for range-of-motion only — heel slides, supported knee bends and ankle pumps. Resume strengthening when the joint is no longer warm or significantly swollen. Aquatic exercise (warm-water hydrotherapy at 33–35 °C) is particularly well tolerated. See our <a href="/conditions/rheumatoid-arthritis/exercises">rheumatoid arthritis exercises guide</a> for flare-friendly routines and pacing rules.</p>

<h2 id="gentle-knee-exercises-severe-arthritis">Gentle Knee Exercises for Severe Arthritis</h2>
<p>If you have severe knee arthritis, are awaiting surgery or struggle to weight-bear, these seated and lying options keep the joint moving without overload: seated knee extensions (slowly straighten the knee from a chair, hold 5 seconds, 10 reps), heel slides (lying on your back, slide the heel towards the buttock), and gentle isometric quad sets (press the back of the knee into a rolled towel for 5 seconds). Two short sessions daily of 10 minutes each are more effective and better tolerated than one long session.</p>

<h2 id="knee-strengthening-exercises-post-surgery">Knee Strengthening Exercises After Knee Replacement</h2>
<p>After a total or partial knee replacement, your hospital physiotherapist will issue a protocol — follow it precisely for the first 6 weeks. The universal early goals are: regain full knee extension (a straight knee), achieve 90° of knee flexion within 2–4 weeks, and rebuild quadriceps activation through straight leg raises and quad sets. Long-term, the strengthening programme on this page is the standard maintenance routine for most post-replacement patients beyond 12 weeks. Always clear new exercises with your surgical team or chartered physiotherapist first.</p>

<h2 id="exercises-to-avoid-knee-arthritis">Exercises to Avoid With Knee Arthritis</h2>
<p>Not all exercise is equal. With knee arthritis, avoid or strictly limit: deep squats below 90°, running on hard surfaces if you have moderate-to-severe OA, high-impact aerobics and jumping, heavy leg extension machines (which load the knee in a vulnerable position), lunges with the front knee travelling far past the toes, and full-depth leg presses. None of these are universally banned, but they are the most common causes of post-exercise flares in our experience. If in doubt, <a href="/uk/london/physiotherapy">find a physiotherapist near you</a> for a personalised assessment.</p>

<h2 id="knee-arthritis-faq">Knee Arthritis Exercise FAQ — People Also Ask</h2>

<h3>What are the best exercises for arthritic knees?</h3>
<p>The best exercises for arthritic knees combine three elements: strengthening (straight leg raises, sit-to-stand, wall sits and step-ups), low-impact aerobic work (stationary cycling, swimming or brisk walking) and daily flexibility (heel slides, hamstring stretches and calf stretches). Done 3–4 times per week, this combination reduces knee pain by 25–50% on average — comparable to the effect of paracetamol or NSAIDs.</p>

<h3>How often should I do knee exercises for arthritis?</h3>
<p>UK physiotherapy guidance recommends strengthening exercises 2–3 times per week, low-impact aerobic exercise 3–5 times per week (30 minutes per session, which can be split into 3 × 10 minutes) and gentle range-of-motion stretches every day. Allow at least one rest day between strengthening sessions to let muscle recover.</p>

<h3>Can exercise cure knee arthritis?</h3>
<p>Exercise cannot reverse cartilage loss or cure knee arthritis, but it is the single most effective <em>non-surgical</em> treatment. A consistent 12-week programme can reduce pain by 25–50%, improve walking distance, delay the need for joint replacement and improve quality of life. The benefits stop if exercise stops — it is a long-term management tool, not a short course.</p>

<h3>What exercises should I avoid with knee arthritis?</h3>
<p>Avoid high-impact activities (running on concrete, jumping, plyometrics), deep squats below 90°, heavy seated leg-extension machines, and lunges where the front knee travels well beyond the toes. Replace them with stationary cycling, swimming, sit-to-stand from a chair, and partial squats to a chair height.</p>

<h3>Are there low-impact knee exercises for severe arthritis?</h3>
<p>Yes. Seated knee extensions, heel slides, isometric quad sets, ankle pumps and warm-water (hydrotherapy) exercise are all suitable for severe knee arthritis. Two short 10-minute sessions per day are usually better tolerated than one longer session, and there is no requirement to weight-bear for the exercises to be effective.</p>

<h3>How long does it take for knee exercises to reduce arthritis pain?</h3>
<p>Most people notice reduced stiffness within 2–3 weeks of consistent practice. Meaningful pain reduction typically appears at 6–8 weeks, with the full benefit of a structured programme seen at 12 weeks. Stopping for more than 2–3 weeks usually reverses the gains.</p>

